`,n.jsxs(e.p,{children:["In astronomy, we hunt for ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Earth-like worlds"})," - places where the same physics supports life under different skies. In software, the parallel is familiar: ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Web, Android, and iOS"})," look like three planets, but users expect the same product on each."]}),`
`,n.jsxs(e.p,{children:["Technology moves quickly, but one principle remains constant: ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"simplicity scales"}),"."]}),`
`,n.jsxs(e.p,{children:["Rather than chasing a new framework every year, I prefer systems that are maintainable, scalable, secure, and easy to evolve. My goal is straightforward - build ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"one shared application core"})," that serves every platform while staying ready for enterprise growth."]}),`
`,n.jsxs(e.p,{children:["This article is my blueprint: the stack I reach for, and the ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"architecture decisions"})," that turn a demo into a platform - monorepos, service boundaries, communication patterns, and the launch infrastructure that keeps releases boring in the best way."]}),`
`,n.jsx(e.h2,{children:"Honest scope: one language first"}),`
`,n.jsxs(e.p,{children:["This article is ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"not"})," claiming TypeScript everywhere is the only valid architecture. It is not a language ranking, and I am not saying I only work in one ecosystem."]}),`
`,n.jsxs(e.p,{children:[n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"The focus here is narrower:"})," ship ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Web, Android, and iOS"})," from a shared codebase - without three client codebases slowly becoming three different products. For that mission, using ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"one language across the surfaces you control"})," helps teams move faster, share types, and steer development without constant context-switching."]}),`
`,n.jsxs(e.p,{children:["That is why I mention ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"NestJS"})," on the backend in this blueprint: it lets the same team work in TypeScript from mobile UI through API modules. It is a ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"pragmatic choice for velocity and consistency"}),", not proof that Node always beats other runtimes."]}),`
`,n.jsxs(e.p,{children:[n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Python is often the stronger backend for other jobs."})," Data pipelines, ML, scientific workloads, and much of the AI ecosystem lean heavily on Python and its libraries - frequently with more depth than the Node ecosystem offers in those areas. I intend to cover Python-centric backend architecture in a ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"separate blog post"}),". This one stays focused on the three-OS, one-language problem."]}),`
`,n.jsxs(e.p,{children:["If your product needs a Python core, you can still apply the patterns here - monorepos, events, IaC, CI/CD - while swapping mission control to FastAPI, Django, or similar. The ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"platform thinking"})," transfers; only the engine room changes."]}),`
