`,n.jsxs(e.p,{children:["For much of the 2000s and early 2010s, many sites followed a familiar pattern: the server rendered HTML, the browser displayed it, and JavaScript improved interactivity where needed. ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"jQuery"})," made that layer approachable across inconsistent browsers. ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"WordPress"}),", ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Drupal"}),", and similar CMS platforms made publishing fast for millions of sites. That stack still works for a large share of the web today."]}),`
`,n.jsxs(e.p,{children:["What changed was ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"user expectations"}),". Search-as-you-type, live dashboards, collaborative editing, and mobile apps raised the bar for interactivity. Teams building long-lived ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"applications"})," — not only articles and brochures — often adopted component-based JavaScript frameworks. ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"React"})," became one of the most widely used options for that job. ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"React Native"})," extended a similar component model to iOS and Android."]}),`
`,n.jsx(e.p,{children:"This article discusses those shifts in neutral terms. jQuery, WordPress, and React solve overlapping but not identical problems. Preferences here reflect the author's experience; your stack should match your product, team skills, and maintenance horizon."}),`
`,n.jsx(e.h2,{children:"What jQuery did well — and where larger apps strained"}),`
`,n.jsxs(e.p,{children:[n.jsx(e.a,{href:"https://jquery.com/",target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",children:"jQuery"})," remains maintained and appropriate for many tasks. It unified DOM APIs, simplified events and AJAX, and helped a generation of developers ship interactivity quickly. For small enhancements on server-rendered pages, it is still a reasonable choice."]}),`
`,n.jsxs(e.p,{children:["Difficulties tended to appear as ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"application complexity"}),' grew. Imperative DOM updates — select an element, mutate it, bind another handler — can scatter state across the page. In large codebases, answering "what is on screen?" may require tracing selectors instead of reading a single state model. That pain point motivated declarative libraries, including React, Vue, and others — not a failure of jQuery itself, but a different scale of problem.']}),`
`,n.jsx(e.div,{className:"blog-table-wrap",children:n.jsxs(e.table,{children:[n.jsx(e.thead,{children:n.jsxs(e.tr,{children:[n.jsx(e.th,{children:"Approach"}),n.jsx(e.th,{children:"Mental model"}),n.jsx(e.th,{children:"Often strong for"}),n.jsx(e.th,{children:"Common scaling challenges"})]})}),n.jsxs(e.tbody,{children:[n.jsxs(e.tr,{children:[n.jsx(e.td,{"data-label":"Approach",children:"jQuery"}),n.jsx(e.td,{"data-label":"Mental model",children:"Enhance the DOM in place"}),n.jsx(e.td,{"data-label":"Often strong for",children:"Progressive enhancement, legacy pages"}),n.jsx(e.td,{"data-label":"Common scaling challenges",children:"State spread across imperative updates"})]}),n.jsxs(e.tr,{children:[n.jsx(e.td,{"data-label":"Approach",children:"Backbone / Knockout"}),n.jsx(e.td,{"data-label":"Mental model",children:"Models with bindings"}),n.jsx(e.td,{"data-label":"Often strong for",children:"Structured medium-sized apps (historical)"}),n.jsx(e.td,{"data-label":"Common scaling challenges",children:"Boilerplate and binding complexity at scale"})]}),n.jsxs(e.tr,{children:[n.jsx(e.td,{"data-label":"Approach",children:"AngularJS (1.x)"}),n.jsx(e.td,{"data-label":"Mental model",children:"Full framework in the page"}),n.jsx(e.td,{"data-label":"Often strong for",children:"Enterprise CRUD (historical)"}),n.jsx(e.td,{"data-label":"Common scaling challenges",children:"Large-app performance and migration paths"})]}),n.jsxs(e.tr,{children:[n.jsx(e.td,{"data-label":"Approach",children:"React"}),n.jsx(e.td,{"data-label":"Mental model",children:"UI as a function of state"}),n.jsx(e.td,{"data-label":"Often strong for",children:"Component-driven product UIs"}),n.jsx(e.td,{"data-label":"Common scaling challenges",children:"Tooling choices and ecosystem breadth"})]})]})]})}),`
`,n.jsx(e.p,{children:"React's declarative model asks: given this state, what should render? That can simplify reviews, tests, and refactors when many people touch the same UI — one reason product teams adopted it, alongside alternatives such as Vue and Angular."}),`
`,n.jsx(e.h2,{children:"WordPress and CMS platforms: different layer, different strength"}),`
`,n.jsxs(e.p,{children:[n.jsx(e.a,{href:"https://wordpress.org/",target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",children:"WordPress"})," powers a large percentage of the public web — particularly for blogs, marketing sites, and small commerce. Themes, plugins, editorial workflows, and SEO tooling remain difficult to match for time-to-value in pure publishing scenarios."]}),`
`,n.jsxs(e.p,{children:["Tension appears when the ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"product is primarily an interactive application"}),": personalised dashboards, complex client validation, real-time data, and bespoke workflows. Teams then often separate ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"content"})," from ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"experience"})," — for example, headless WordPress (or another CMS) for editorial data, with React or another framework for the front end. WordPress has also embraced block editing and modern JavaScript in its own ecosystem."]}),`
`,n.jsxs(e.p,{children:["React did not make WordPress obsolete. The two frequently ",n.jsx(e.strong,{children:"coexist"}),": WordPress for content operations, React for rich client surfaces where that split makes sense."]}),`
`,n.jsx(e.h2,{children:"Why React gained wide adoption in product engineering"}),`
